 "voice": "Adam",
 "voice_id": "pNInz6obpgDQGcFmaJgB",
 "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "text": "You run K-Means clustering on a dataset with three clearly visible groups of points. You set K equal to three and run the algorithm twice using different random seeds.",
   "char_start": 0,
   "char_end": 167,
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 11.029
  },
  {
   "text": "The first run identifies all three groups correctly. The second run splits one group into two clusters and merges the other two groups together.",
   "char_start": 169,
   "char_end": 313,
   "start": 11.471,
   "end": 19.946
  },
  {
   "text": "Both runs converge, and neither produces any further changes to its cluster assignments.",
   "char_start": 315,
   "char_end": 403,
   "start": 20.387,
   "end": 25.739
  },
  {
   "text": "Why does K-Means produce different results on the exact same dataset?",
   "char_start": 405,
   "char_end": 474,
   "start": 26.181,
   "end": 30.209
  },
  {
   "text": "A. K-Means minimizes a non-convex objective, so different initial centroids can converge to different local minima.",
   "char_start": 476,
   "char_end": 591,
   "start": 30.592,
   "end": 38.974,
   "option": "A"
  },
  {
   "text": "B. K-Means automatically changes the number of clusters during optimization.",
   "char_start": 593,
   "char_end": 669,
   "start": 39.357,
   "end": 44.152,
   "option": "B"
  },
  {
   "text": "C. K-Means uses a different distance metric each time it runs.",
   "char_start": 671,
   "char_end": 733,
   "start": 44.594,
   "end": 48.947,
   "option": "C"
  },
  {
   "text": "D. K-Means guarantees the global optimum, but only when the dataset contains fewer than three clusters.",
   "char_start": 735,
   "char_end": 838,
   "start": 49.33,
   "end": 56.262,
   "option": "D"
  },
  {
   "text": "Which is it, and how would you confirm it experimentally using different initializations and inertia scores?",
   "char_start": 840,
   "char_end": 948,
   "start": 56.645,
   "end": 63.111
  }
 ],
